<topic xml:id="rt-shielding-resources-creating-shield"
 role="task" xml:lang="en"
 xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.2"
 xmlns:its="http://www.w3.org/2005/11/its"
 xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
 xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
 xmlns:trans="http://docbook.org/ns/transclusion">
  <info>
    <title>Setting up a CPU shield using &systemd;</title><!-- can be changed via merge
in the assembly -->
    <!-- add author's e-mail -->
    <meta name="maintainer" content="jsindelarova@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
    <abstract><!-- can be changed via merge in the assembly -->
      <para>
       The general idea is to have one cpuset for the main sensitive workload and a complementary cpuset for the supporting tasks. Resources are distributed in the top-down fashion, so to ensure proper allocation for the main workload we must take into consideration all the top-level cgroups on the system. &systemd; by default creates the following units: <filename>init.scope</filename>, <filename>system.slice</filename>, <filename>user.slice</filename>, and <filename>machine.slice</filename>.
      </para>
    </abstract>
  </info>
  <procedure xml:id="pro-setup-cpu-shield">
    <title>Setting up systemd CPU shield drop-in files</title>
    <step>
      <para>
        Create drop-in configuration files for <filename>init.scope</filename>, <filename>system.slice</filename>,
        <filename>user.slice</filename>, and <filename>machine.slice</filename> to restrict them to non-shielded CPUs.
      </para>
      <itemizedlist>
      <listitem>
      <para>For the <filename>init.scope</filename>, run:</para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/init.scope.d/40-shielding.conf</command>
[Scope]
AllowedCPUs=0-1
</screen>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
      <para>For the <filename>system.slice</filename>, run:</para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/system.slice.d/40-shielding.conf</command>
[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=0-1
</screen>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
      <para>For the <filename>user.slice</filename>, run:</para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/user.slice.d/40-shielding.conf</command>
[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=0-1
</screen>
      </listitem>
      <listitem>
      <para>For the <filename>machine.slice</filename>, run:</para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/machine.slice.d/40-shielding.conf</command>
[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=0-1
</screen>
      </listitem>
      </itemizedlist> 
    </step>
    <step>
      <para>
        Create a custom workload slice file at <filename>/etc/systemd/system/workload.slice</filename> assigned to the shielded CPUs.
      </para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /etc/systemd/system/workload.slice</command>
[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=2-15</screen>
    </step>
    <step>
      <para>
        Reload the systemd manager configuration to apply the new unit files.
      </para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>systemctl daemon-reload</command></screen>
    </step>
    <step>
      <para>
        Verify the CPU assignment by checking the <filename>cpuset.cpus</filename> file in the cgroup filesystem.
      </para>
      <screen>&prompt.sudo;<command>cat /sys/fs/cgroup/workload.slice/cpuset.cpus</command>
2-15</screen>
    </step>
  </procedure>
</topic>
